#strong[Problema:] Calcule el calor total necesario para calentar $50.0 thin upright("g")$ de agua líquida desde $20.0^compose upright("C")$ hasta convertirla en vapor a $100.0^compose upright("C")$. Datos: $c_(p\,upright("agua")) = 4.184 thin upright("J/(g") dot.op^compose upright("C)")$, $Delta H_(upright("vap")) = 2260 thin upright("J/g")$.

#strong[Paso 1:] Calor sensible para calentar el líquido de $20^compose upright("C")$ a $100^compose upright("C")$: $ q_1 = m dot.op c_p dot.op Delta T = 50.0 thin upright("g") dot.op 4.184 thin frac(upright("J"), upright("g") dot.op^compose upright("C")) dot.op\(100.0 - 20.0\)thin zws^compose upright("C") = 16736 thin upright("J") = 16.74 thin upright("kJ") $ #strong[Paso 2:] Calor latente de vaporización a $100^compose upright("C")$: $ q_2 = m dot.op Delta H_(upright("vap")) = 50.0 thin upright("g") dot.op 2260 thin upright("J") / upright("g") = 113000 thin upright("J") = 113.00 thin upright("kJ") $ #strong[Paso 3:] Calor total acumulado: $ q_(upright("total")) = q_1 + q_2 = 16.74 thin upright("kJ") + 113.00 thin upright("kJ") = 129.74 thin upright("kJ") $

